  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1226 PM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nMonday...The unusually mild weather continues with broad southwest \nflow aloft, and high 500 mb heights near 5760 meters overhead. This \nwill induce decent surface lee troughing and mild weather \nenthusiasts will rejoice with 700 mb temperatures near 6 Celsius and \ndownslope warming boosting maximum temperatures into the mid \n50s to mid 60s.\n\nTuesday...Stronger southwest flow aloft develops across our neck of \nthe woods here in southeast Wyoming and western Nebraska, with a \nslightly negatively tilted shortwave trough aloft moving across \nIdaho, Utah and western Wyoming, with surface lee troughing keeping \nour weather conditions high and dry once again with slightly cooler \n700 mb temperatures near 4 Celsius and slightly cooler temperatures \nfor our forecast area, though still on the mild side.\n\nWednesday...The aforementioned shortwave trough aloft loses\namplitude as it moves to eastern Montana and the western \nDakotas by sunrise, sending its associated cold front into our \ncounties, Pacific originated. As is typical with these systems, \nwill see scattered to numerous rain and snow showers early in \nthe day west of a Douglas to Laramie line due to dynamics and \norographics with some decent snows across our northern Laramie, \nSnowy and Sierra Madre Ranges, though downslope wind east of the\nmountains and Laramie Range will as usual provide a rain shadow\neffect with little if any precipitation east of the Interstate \n25 corridor and Laramie Range. Orographic snows will continue in\nthe afternoon for our Snowy and Sierra Madre Ranges, and we may\nneed some type of Winter Weather Highlight such as a Winter \nWeather Advisory, if model congruency continues.\n\nThursday...Looks like a windy and relatively mild day for November, \nthe beginning of our typical windy November through March windy \nseason. Strong southwest flow aloft continues, and our wind prone \nlocations such as Arlington, Bordeaux and the Interstate 80 Summit \nbetween Laramie and Cheyenne may see some strong winds, and possible \nwind highlights.\n\nFriday...For climatology enthusiast, November 20th is the \nlatest first measurable snowfall event in recorded history for \nCheyenne, with at least one tenth of an inch of snow measured on\nthe ground. Interestingly, this coincides with a decent and \nprogressive neutrally tilted shortwave trough aloft that is \nprogged to move across our forecast area with a rather strong \ncold frontal passage, which if model timing is correct will \nproduce decent orographic snows for our Laramie, Snowy and \nSierra Madre Ranges, and some likely accumulating snow for lower\nelevations due to the thickness packing along the front, \ndynamics and synoptic forcing, especially if the track of the \nsystem is a bit further south across Colorado, rather than over \nWyoming as currently depicted, so please stay tuned to the \nlatest forecasts from your entire National Weather Service team.\nIn any event. Friday the 20th, will be a cold and blustery day \nwith measurable snow becoming increasingly likely at many \nlocations, although snowfall amounts are quite unpredictable for\nday 7, one week out. Maximum temperatures will only be in the \nupper 30s to upper 40s with breezy to windy conditions, only six\ndays before Thanksgiving.",
